
02. ASTEROID COLLISION


1. Problem Statement

Given an array of asteroids, determine which asteroids survive after all collisions.

Positive values move right and negative values move left. The absolute value represents the size. The smaller asteroid is destroyed, and if both sizes are equal, both are destroyed.

Example:
Input: [10, 2, -5]
Output: [10]

2. Brute-Force Approach

Repeatedly find adjacent asteroids moving toward each other, resolve their collision, and repeat until no collisions remain.

Complexity:
Time: O(n^2)
Auxiliary Space: O(n)

3. Optimized Approach: Stack

Use a stack to store surviving asteroids. A collision occurs only when the stack top is positive and the incoming asteroid is negative.

Code:

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

vector<int> asteroidCollision(vector<int>& asteroids) {
    vector<int> st;

    for (int cur : asteroids) {
        bool alive = true;

        while (!st.empty() && st.back() > 0 && cur < 0) {
            long long topSize = st.back();
            long long curSize = -(long long)cur;

            if (topSize < curSize) {
                st.pop_back();
                continue;
            }

            if (topSize == curSize) {
                st.pop_back();
            }

            alive = false;
            break;
        }

        if (alive) {
            st.push_back(cur);
        }
    }

    return st;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> asteroids = {10, 2, -5};
    vector<int> ans = asteroidCollision(asteroids);

    for (int x : ans) {
        cout << x << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}

4. Dry Run

Input: [10, 2, -5]

Step 1: Push 10 -> [10]
Step 2: Push 2 -> [10, 2]
Step 3: -5 destroys 2 -> [10]
Step 4: -5 loses to 10 -> [10]

Output: [10]

5. Complexity

Time: O(n)
Auxiliary Space: O(n)

Each asteroid is pushed once and popped at most once.

6. Edge Cases

[8, -8] -> []
[5, -10] -> [-10]
[-2, -1, 1, 2] -> [-2, -1, 1, 2]
[1, 2, 3, -10] -> [-10]
[-2, 2] -> [-2, 2]
